=== task: mcq medium
title: Finding followers
prompt: A follow graph has V people and E follows, stored as lists of who each person follows. Without any other structure, how long does it take to list the followers of one person?
* O(V + E): every list has to be checked :: Right. The edges arriving at a person can be in anyone's list.
- O(1) :: That's the time to get the list of people someone follows, not their followers.
- O(out-degree) :: Reading their own list gives who they follow.
- O(log V) :: Nothing is sorted or arranged to allow a quicker search.
hint: Where are the edges arriving at a person stored?
hint: How many lists might contain that person?
right: O(V + E). Building the reverse graph once, also O(V + E), turns every later followers look-up into a single dictionary access.
wrong: Followers are the in-neighbours, and they're spread across every list, so finding them means looking at all of them.

=== task: predict medium
title: Directed cycles
code:
```python
def is_directed_cycle(g, route):
    if len(route) < 3 or route[0] != route[-1]:
        return False
    for i in range(len(route) - 1):
        if route[i + 1] not in g[route[i]]:
            return False
    return True

g = {"A": ["B"], "B": ["C"], "C": ["A", "D"], "D": ["B"]}
print(is_directed_cycle(g, ["A", "B", "C", "A"]), is_directed_cycle(g, ["A", "C", "B", "A"]))
print(is_directed_cycle(g, ["B", "C", "D", "B"]), is_directed_cycle(g, ["C", "D", "B"]))
```
wrong[True True\nTrue False]: A → C isn't an edge (only C → A is), so going round the other way fails.
wrong[True False\nFalse False]: B → C, C → D and D → B are all edges: that's a cycle too.
wrong[True False\nTrue True]: C, D, B doesn't come back to C, so it's a path, not a cycle.
hint: Every step must follow an arrow forwards.
hint: A cycle must end where it started.
right: A → B → C → A and B → C → D → B follow the arrows back to the start. The reversed loop uses edges that don't exist, and C, D, B never returns.
wrong: Check each step against the lists: A, B, C, A works; A, C fails at A → C; B, C, D, B works; C, D, B doesn't end at C.

